WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.705
A good profile starts with a clear intelligence question.

2
00:00:03.705 --> 00:00:15.878
The team should define what decision the profile will support, which environment or sector is in scope, what data sources are authorized, what time period is relevant, and who the audience is.

3
00:00:15.878 --> 00:00:21.170
A profile written for detection engineers may emphasize behaviors and telemetry.

4
00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:29.638
A profile written for executives may emphasize likely objectives, business risk, confidence, and recommended readiness actions.

5
00:00:29.638 --> 00:00:33.570
Ethical and legal boundaries are part of the analytic method.

6
00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:38.560
Defensive profiling should use authorized data sources and protect privacy.

7
00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.685
It should avoid doxxing, harassment, vigilantism, retaliation, or unsupported accusations.

8
00:00:44.685 --> 00:00:51.792
Analysts should distinguish groups, campaigns, personas, infrastructure clusters, and individual people.

9
00:00:51.792 --> 00:01:00.109
Those categories can overlap in public reporting, but treating them as identical can create serious analytic and ethical errors.

10
00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:07.367
Responsible profiling focuses on defensible behavior patterns rather than speculation or sensational labels.

11
00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:17.952
If the evidence shows repeated targeting of a sector, common infrastructure habits, or recurring operational timing, that may support a useful defensive judgment.

12
00:01:17.952 --> 00:01:24.001
If the evidence only shows a name repeated in several secondary reports, that may not be enough.

13
00:01:24.001 --> 00:01:31.411
Scope keeps the work grounded: what are we trying to understand, using which evidence, for which defensive purpose?

14
00:01:31.411 --> 00:01:34.284
AI systems should inherit those boundaries.

15
00:01:34.284 --> 00:01:47.364
Prompts should direct the model to separate facts from judgments, include source references, flag uncertainty, and avoid claims about real people unless the evidence and policy support that discussion.

16
00:01:47.364 --> 00:01:56.286
The model should not be asked to invent actor names, infer private identities, or turn ambiguous observations into confident attribution.

17
00:01:56.286 --> 00:02:01.427
Ethics and analytic rigor are not separate concerns; they reinforce each other.
